NOTE on the table above: /api/tools/merge-pdf answering 404 is correct — merge is a
client-side tool ({"error":"This tool does not support server processing."}). The
server-tool control is /api/tools/compress-pdf, which answers 400 on a bogus body.

================================================================================
WHAT THIS MEASURES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
================================================================================

The A/B that isolates the matcher. `/api/nope` and `/api/nope.txt` both resolve to
NO route handler — the only difference between them is that the second ends in a
dot, so `.*\.[^/]*$` excludes it from the proxy matcher. Matched: 64 MiB read.
Excluded: 1.25 MiB read, then 404. With the handler held constant at "none", that
63 MiB is attributable to matcher membership and nothing else. It is the most
direct evidence in this branch that excluding the five upload paths is
load-bearing rather than cosmetic.

Mechanism, read rather than inferred. `node_modules/next/dist/server/body-streams.js`
`cloneBodyStream()` feeds two PassThroughs while counting bytes; past
`proxyClientMaxBodySize` it pushes null into both and RETURNS EARLY on every
later chunk — it never unpipes `input`. So the cap bounds what is RETAINED, not
what is read off the socket: the full body is drained either way. `finalize()`
then swaps the buffered clone in as the request body, which is why the cap has to
stay above every advertised ceiling (next.config.mjs:132-158 records the
measurement: 10481664 bytes went through, 10485760 did not).

The residue scales with concurrency and is not returned:

    concurrency   baseline   peak      delta     6s after
    4 x 100 MiB    301 MB     562 MB   +261 MB    579 MB
    8 x 100 MiB    577 MB     951 MB   +374 MB    984 MB
   16 x 100 MiB    984 MB    1728 MB   +744 MB   1795 MB

28 anonymous requests to a page URL took one process from 301 MB to 1795 MB. Per
request it is bounded (120 MiB, the configured clone limit); in aggregate it is
bounded only by concurrency, and no rate limiter stands in front of a page path —
`checkUploadLimit` is wired into `workspaceUploadGate` on the five upload routes
and TOOLS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN into the tool routes.

NOT CAUSED BY THIS BRANCH. The matcher has always claimed page paths; the
exclusion only REMOVED five paths from it. Every large-body route the product
advertises — the three uploads, /api/tools/[slug], /api/jobs — is already
excluded, so on today's artifact the 120 MiB clone limit governs no legitimate
body at all. It is retained deliberately (next.config.mjs:150-157) as the
fallback should one of those paths ever be matched again.

NOT FIXED HERE, and the reason is a trade-off that is the owner's to make.
Lowering `proxyClientMaxBodySize` is the only in-app lever, and it re-arms the
silent-truncation footgun that comment records for any future matched body route.
The zero-cost lever is at the edge, which SERVER_SETUP.md does not mention at
all today: a small `client_max_body_size` by default, raised only on the same
path set the matcher excludes. Both are written up in the report; neither is
taken unilaterally.

Also measured, and correct product behaviour rather than a finding:
POST /api/tools/merge-pdf answers 404 {"error":"This tool does not support server
processing."} because merge is client-side. The server-tool control is
/api/tools/compress-pdf, which answers 400 on a bogus body.

The two probe scripts beside this log are kept as `.mjs.txt`, not `.mjs`. As
`.mjs` they were inside eslint's default file set and took the lint gate from
0 errors to 20 (`no-undef` on Buffer/process/console — evidence scripts are not
covered by the `scripts/` globals override). Retained evidence that breaks a
gate is not evidence; rename to `.mjs` to run them.
